<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tudhaliya I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 15th–14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tudhaliya I was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, usually dated to the late 15th or early 14th century BCE, at the start of the period often called the Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. His annals record campaigns in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, including the defeat of the Assuwa coalition of 22 lands, whose captured soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>. Scholars disagree on whether he and a Tudhaliya II were one ruler or two.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-i.jpg" alt="Tudhaliya I" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA; colorized and wording by Laszlovszky András at Hungarian Wikipedia based on Világtörténet tíz kötetben, Kossuth... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C3%A9rk%C3%A9p_Tudhalijasz_%C3%A9s_Arnuvandasz_kor%C3%A1r%C3%B3l.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
